# A Guided Laboratory for Local-to-Global Data

A blank cell and a contradiction are not the same thing. Begin with the completion explorer and observe that every unconstrained partial table has at least one completion.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

Run the matching numerical construction:

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the completion theorem and its proof idea</summary>
For a cell set $I$, nonempty value set $V$, observed set $O$, and observations $y:O\to V$, select $v_0\in V$. Keep $y(i)$ on $O$ and assign $v_0$ elsewhere. If all cells are observed the extension is unique; if one is blank and two values exist, changing that cell gives two completions.
</details>

## Overlaps are not automatically independent

Use the rank laboratory to compare a path with a graph containing many cycles.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

The exact counting procedure is available here:

{{algorithm:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the graph-counting argument</summary>
Equality propagates along paths, so every feasible assignment is constant on each connected component. Conversely, choosing one of $q$ values independently on each of $c$ components constructs every feasible assignment exactly once. Hence there are $q^c$ feasible assignments and, among $q^N$ uniform assignments, probability $q^{c-N}$.
</details>

For additional background, see [sheaf theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sheaf_(mathematics)), [connected components](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Connected_component_(graph_theory)), and [disjoint-set data structures](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Disjoint-set_data_structure).

## Optimization under consistency

Now explore how a connected component chooses a consensus value that minimizes observed mismatches.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

{{algorithm:0}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the minimization proof</summary>
A feasible assignment is constant on each component. Assigning value $v$ to component $C$ incurs $|C\cap O|-n_C(v)$ mismatches, where $n_C(v)$ is the observed frequency of $v$. Thus a mode minimizes component loss. Since total loss is the sum over components, choosing a mode independently in every component is globally optimal.
</details>

## Probability in the right coordinates

The next plot replaces raw edge count by the invariant that matters: connected-component count.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:1}}

<details><summary>Why the proposed missing-rate formula fails</summary>
Under the unconstrained partial-function model, every missingness mask is completable, so completability probability is $1$. A formula $(1-r)^C$ instead measures a product of observation-like events and cannot be universal. A valid probability theorem must specify random values, missingness, noise, and the independent rank of compatibility constraints.
</details>

## Comparing methods responsibly

No observation-preserving method can be strictly superior on every instance with missing rate below one half: the range includes zero missingness, where all such methods return the complete truth and tie at zero loss. Meaningful comparisons therefore concern expected risk under explicit data-generating assumptions rather than universal strict dominance.

